---
description: Never define React components inside other components or render callbacks
globs: **/*.tsx,**/*.jsx
alwaysApply: true
---

# No components created during render

Never declare a React component (function/const returning JSX with a
**Capitalized** name) inside another component body, a render prop, a
hook, or any other function that runs during render.

Each call creates a **new component type**. React treats it as a different
element, remounts the subtree every time, resets local state, and wastes
work.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — new `Btn` type every renderMoveActions call
renderMoveActions: (actions) => {
  const Btn = ({ label, onClick }) => <button …>{label}</button>;
  return <Btn label="↑" onClick={actions.up} />;
};

// ❌ BAD — nested inside a parent component
const Parent = () => {
  const Row = () => <div />;
  return <Row />;
};

// ✅ GOOD — stable module-scope component
const Btn = ({ label, onClick }: { … }) => (
  <button type="button" onClick={onClick ?? undefined} disabled={!onClick}>
    {label}
  </button>
);

renderMoveActions: (actions) => (
  <span>
    <Btn label="↑" onClick={actions.up} />
  </span>
);
```

## What to do instead

1. Hoist the child to **module scope** (same file is fine).
2. Or reuse an existing shared chrome helper (e.g. demo `MoveBar` / `Btn`).
3. If you only need markup once, **inline the JSX** — do not wrap it in a
   nested capitalized function just to shorten the call site.

Helpers that return ReactNode and are **not** used as `<Helper />` (plain
functions called as `helper()`) are fine; the bug is specifically
**`<Capitalized />` whose type is re-created each render**.
